My current workaround: For each section you are interested in, register an internal setting with some magic key and store in it the section and an identifier for the object e.g. in my case `application/loading_screen/_htk_section_marker="papplication/loading_screen"` -> the "p" means project settings, the rest of the string is the section. Also register this value as default value, to prevent the setting from getting serialized.

The relevant information can then be reconstructed:
```gdscript
func _can_handle(object: Object) -> bool:
    if object.get_class() != "SectionedInspectorFilter":
        return false
    return typeof(object.property_get_revert("_htk_section_marker")) == TYPE_STRING

func _parse_property(...) -> bool:
    var marker := object.property_get_revert(SECTION_MARKER) as String
    var canonical_object: Object = ProjectSettings if marker[0] == "p" else EditorInterface.get_editor_settings()
    var canonical_name := marker.substr(1) + "/" + name
```

But the workaround runs the risk of accidentally serializing the setting if something goes wrong.
